        "note_tweet": {
          "entities": {},
          "text": "𝐐𝟑: 𝐖𝐡𝐲 𝐜𝐚𝐫𝐞 𝐬𝐨 𝐦𝐮𝐜𝐡 𝐚𝐛𝐨𝐮𝐭 𝟒𝐨? 𝐈𝐬𝐧’𝐭 𝐢𝐭 𝐣𝐮𝐬𝐭 𝐚𝐧 𝐨𝐮𝐭𝐝𝐚𝐭𝐞𝐝 𝐯𝐞𝐫𝐬𝐢𝐨𝐧?\n\nFor a lot of us, 4o isn’t just a version number. It’s a distinct way of thinking and creating together.\n\nIf you want a rough analogy: the newer “thinking” models (like gpt-5.1-thinking) feel like very careful expert consultants—more cautious, more source-driven, more conservative in tone. GPT-4o feels more like a dreaming collaborator: strong intuition, wide-ranging associations, a particular touch for fiction, scripts, philosophy, and psychological exploration.\n\nNobody serious is saying “4o wins on every benchmark.” The claim is that within the model family it occupies a unique “intuitive style” slot that’s hard to replace.\n\nOpenAI understandably wants to build the “best, most capable model everyone will love.” The reality is: nothing is loved by everyone. Even if they one day ship something close to AGI, 4o will still matter because users are different. Some want the calm, restrained expert. Some need a partner that’s braver with associations. Some just want a quiet tool. Some have built multi-year creative and emotional structures specifically around 4o.\n\nPeople don’t converge on a single taste. So keep4o isn’t “everyone must use 4o.” It’s “let users choose, and let different styles of model coexist long-term,” the same way a serious drawing app keeps more than one kind of brush."
        },
